== Why Saints Persevere

#smallcaps[The] hope which filled Paul’s heart for the Corinthian brethren is, as we have already seen, full of comfort to those who tremble about their future. But why did he believe that the brethren would be confirmed to the end?

I want you to notice that he gives his reasons. Here they are:

#quote[“God is faithful, by whom you#super[+] were called into the fellowship of His Son Jesus Christ.” — 1 Cor 1:9.]

The apostle does not say, “#emph[You#super[+]] are faithful.” Alas! the faithfulness of man is a very unreliable thing; it is mere vanity. He does not say, “You#super[+] have faithful ministers to lead and guide you, and so I trust you will be safe.” Oh, no! if we are kept by men, we will be poorly kept. He puts it, “God is faithful.” If we are found faithful, it will be because God is faithful. On the faithfulness of our covenant God the whole weight of our salvation must rest. On this glorious attribute of God the matter hinges. We are as changeable as the wind, as frail as a spider’s web, as weak as water. No reliance can be placed on our natural qualities, or our spiritual attainments; but God remains faithful. He is faithful in his love; he knows no change, nor shadow of turning. He is faithful to his purpose; he does not begin a work and then leave it undone. He is faithful to his relationships; as a Father he will not disown his children, as a friend he will not deny his people, as a Creator he will not forsake the work of his own hands. He is faithful to his promises, and will never allow one of them to fail a single believer. He is faithful to his covenant, which he has made with us in Christ Jesus, and ratified with the blood of his sacrifice. He is faithful to his Son, and will not allow his precious blood to be spilled in vain. He is faithful to his people, to whom he has promised eternal life, and from whom he will not turn away.

This faithfulness of God is the foundation and cornerstone of our hope of final perseverance. The saints will persevere in holiness, because God perseveres in grace. He perseveres in blessing, and so believers persevere in being blessed. He continues to keep his people, and so they continue to keep his commandments. This is good solid ground to rest on, and it fits delightfully with the motto of this little book, “#smallcaps[all of grace].” So it is free favor and infinite mercy which ring in the dawn of salvation, and the same sweet bells ring out melodiously through the whole day of grace.

You see that the only reasons for hoping that we will be confirmed to the end, and be found blameless at the last, are found in our God; but in him these reasons are exceedingly abundant.

They lie first #emph[in what God has done]. He has gone so far in blessing us that he cannot possibly turn back. Paul reminds us that he has “called us into the fellowship of His Son Jesus Christ.” Has he called us? Then the call cannot be reversed; for “God’s gifts and His call are without repentance.” The Lord never turns back from the effectual call of his grace. “Those He called, He also justified; those He justified, He also glorified”: this is the unvarying rule of God’s way of working. There is a common call, of which it is said, “Many are called, but few are chosen”; but the call we are now thinking of is another kind, which signals special love, and makes it certain that we will possess what we are called to. In such a case it is with the one called just as with Abraham’s seed, of whom the Lord said, “I have called you from the ends of the earth, and said to you, ‘You are My servant’; I have chosen you and not rejected you.”

In what the Lord has done we see strong reasons for our preservation and future glory, because the Lord has called us #emph[into the fellowship of his Son Jesus Christ]. It means into partnership with Jesus Christ, and I want you to consider carefully what this means. If you really are called by divine grace, you have come into fellowship with the Lord Jesus Christ, so as to be joint owner with him in all things. From now on you are one with him in the sight of the Most High. The Lord Jesus bore your sins in his own body on the tree, being made a curse for you; and at the same time he has become your righteousness, so that you are justified in him. You are Christ’s, and Christ is yours. As Adam stood for his descendants, so Jesus stands for all who are in him. As husband and wife are one, so Jesus is one with all those who are united to him by faith; one by a marriage union which can never be broken. More than this, believers are members of the body of Christ, and so are one with him by a loving, living, lasting union. God has called us into this union, this fellowship, this partnership, and by this very fact he has given us the token and pledge that we will be confirmed to the end. If we were considered apart from Christ, we would be poor perishable units, soon dissolved and carried away to destruction; but as one with Jesus we are made partakers of his nature, and are endowed with his immortal life. Our destiny is linked with that of our Lord, and until #emph[he] can be destroyed it is not possible that we should perish.

Dwell much on this partnership with the Son of God, to which you have been called: for all your hope lies there. You can never be poor while Jesus is rich, since you are in one firm with him. Need can never overtake you, since you are joint owner with him who is Possessor of heaven and earth. You can never fail; for though one of the partners in the firm is as poor as a church mouse, and in himself an utter bankrupt, who could not pay half a cent on the dollar of his heavy debts, yet the other partner is inconceivably, inexhaustibly rich. In such a partnership you are raised above the depression of the times, the changes of the future, and the shock of the end of all things. The Lord has called you into the fellowship of his Son Jesus Christ, and by that act and deed he has put you in a place of unfailing safety.

If you really are a believer, you are one with Jesus, and therefore you are secure. Do you not see that it must be so? You must be confirmed to the end, until the day of his appearing, if you have indeed been made one with Jesus by the irrevocable act of God. Christ and the believing sinner are in the same boat: unless Jesus sinks, the believer will never drown. Jesus has taken his redeemed into such union with himself that he must first be struck down, overcome, and dishonored, before the least of those he purchased can be harmed. His name is at the head of the firm, and until it can be dishonored we are secure against all dread of failure.

So then, with the utmost confidence let us go forward into the unknown future, linked eternally with Jesus. If the men of the world cry, “Who is this coming up from the wilderness, leaning on her Beloved?” we will joyfully confess that we do lean on Jesus, and that we mean to lean on him more and more. Our faithful God is an ever-flowing well of delight, and our fellowship with the Son of God is a full river of joy. Knowing these glorious things, we cannot be discouraged: no, rather we cry with the apostle, “Who shall separate us from the love of God that is in Christ Jesus our Lord?”
